'''Mie Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|三重県}}, ''Mie-ken'') is a [[Prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located in the [[Miseto Region]] on the island of [[Yagikoshu]], directly east of the [[Toyozawa Metropolis|Toyozawa]] metropolitan area. With a population of 1,033,184 as of 2026, it is one of the smaller prefectures on Yagikoshu by population but has experienced significant growth in recent decades as a {{nihongo|"bedtown prefecture"|ベッドタウン県|beddotaun-ken}} for workers commuting to Toyozawa.

Mie Prefecture's economy is defined by its relationship with Toyozawa. The prefecture serves two primary functions: as a residential hinterland for workers employed in Toyozawa's technology, finance, and service sectors who seek more affordable housing and a quieter lifestyle; and as an industrial satellite hosting manufacturing facilities that require more space than is available in the densely developed Toyozawa metropolitan core.

The {{nihongo|'''Mie Industrial Zone'''|三重工業地帯|Mie Kōgyō Chitai}} is one of the largest manufacturing clusters in the Miseto Region, producing automotive components, consumer electronics, food products, and industrial machinery for the Toyozawa market and for export through the [[Port of Toyozawa]]. The prefecture is also known for its tea production, with the {{nihongo|''Mie-cha''|三重茶|Mie tea}} brand recognised as one of Okaiken's premium tea varieties.

== Geography ==

Mie Prefecture occupies a coastal plain on the eastern edge of the [[Miseto Region]], bounded by [[Toyozawa Metropolis]] to the west, [[Ise Prefecture]] to the east, the [[Suzuka Mountains]] to the south, and the [[Katsuran Sea]] to the north. The prefecture's flat coastal topography and proximity to Toyozawa (approximately 40 km by rail from the capital [[Mie]] to [[Toyozawa Central Station]]) have made it an ideal location for both residential development and industrial facilities requiring large land areas.

The [[Mie River]] flows through the prefecture from its source in the Suzuka Mountains, providing water for agriculture and industry. The river's alluvial plain supports the prefecture's tea plantations and rice paddies.

