== Economy ==

Mie Prefecture's economy is built on three pillars: manufacturing (particularly industries requiring large facilities that cannot be accommodated in Toyozawa's dense urban core), residential services for commuters, and agriculture (particularly tea production).

=== Manufacturing and the Mie Industrial Zone ===
[[File:Mie Industrial Zone.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The Mie Industrial Zone, housing manufacturing facilities for the Toyozawa metropolitan area]]
The {{nihongo|'''Mie Industrial Zone'''|三重工業地帯|Mie Kōgyō Chitai}} is a 25-kilometre strip of manufacturing facilities extending from the city of Mie to the prefecture's western border with Toyozawa Metropolis. The Zone was established in the 1970s as part of a regional development plan to decentralise industry from the overcrowded Toyozawa metropolitan core, and it has since become one of the largest manufacturing clusters in the Miseto Region.

The Zone's industries include:

Industrial production within the zone is organized across four specialized manufacturing sectors. In automotive manufacturing, the [[Suzuki Manufacturing]] complex operates three shifts with roughly 6,800 employees, producing precision transmissions, engine blocks, and electronic sub-assemblies for [[Yonezawa Motors]] and independent assemblers. The [[Mie Electronics Park]] houses high-throughput assembly lines producing consumer audio systems, visual displays, and home appliances for [[Kizan Systems]]. Agricultural processing is concentrated in the [[Mie Food Processing Cluster]], which employs 4,200 workers in tea roasting, cryogenic vegetable freezing, and ready-to-eat packaged meal formulation. Supporting these operations is the [[Godo Machinery District]], which manufactures heavy machine tools, automated welding robots, and foundry equipment for industrial clients across Okaiken.

=== The commuter economy ===
Approximately 35% of Mie Prefecture's working population commutes to Toyozawa for employment, making the prefecture one of the most commuter-dependent in Okaiken. The [[Mie Commuter Rail Line]], operated by [[Okaiken Railways Group]], provides frequent service to Toyozawa Central Station (approximately 40 minutes), and the prefecture's residential communities are designed around commuter needs, with high-density housing near rail stations and extensive park-and-ride facilities.

The commuter economy has driven significant residential development in Mie, with new housing complexes, shopping centres, and schools constructed to serve the growing population of Toyozawa workers seeking affordable housing. The average home price in Mie is approximately 60% of the average in Toyozawa, making the prefecture attractive to young families and first-time homebuyers.

=== Tea production ===
[[File:Mie Tea Terraces.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Mie-cha tea terraces, producing approximately 12,000 tonnes annually]]
{{Main|Mie tea}}
The {{nihongo|'''Mie-cha'''|三重茶|Mie tea}} is one of Okaiken's premium tea varieties, grown in the foothills of the Suzuka Mountains where the volcanic soil and morning mists create ideal growing conditions. The prefecture produces approximately 12,000 tonnes of tea annually, primarily {{nihongo|''sencha''|煎茶|steamed green tea}} and {{nihongo|''matcha''|抹茶|powdered green tea}} for the domestic market and export.

The [[Mie Tea Cooperative]] oversees quality standards and marketing, and the annual [[Mie Tea Festival]] in May draws approximately 45,000 visitors for tea ceremonies, tastings, and cultural events.
Mie's industrial corridor maintains close technological synchronization with the central keiretsu networks of Harujima. The prefecture's ceramic and chemical plants manufacture specialized high-voltage ceramic insulators, synthetic quartz resonators, and dielectric substrates required for subsea power cabling and radar installations. In the surrounding bays, cooperatives harvest cultured spiny lobsters and oysters, balancing heavy automated industrial production with sustainable coastal aquaculture.

== Transportation ==

Mie Prefecture is connected to the [[Minami Yagikoshu Shinkansen]] network via [[Mie Station]], approximately 40 minutes from [[Toyozawa Central Station]]. The [[Mie Commuter Rail Line]] provides frequent service for workers commuting to Toyozawa.

The [[Mie Expressway]] connects the prefecture to Toyozawa and the national expressway network, and the [[Mie Bypass]] provides a route for heavy goods vehicles serving the Industrial Zone.

== Education ==

Mie Prefecture hosts several institutions serving both the local population and commuters:
* [[Mie Prefectural University]] — public university focusing on engineering and business
* [[Mie Institute of Technology]] — engineering and manufacturing technology
* [[Mie Junior College]] — two-year college with vocational programs

